  <!-- Gboard (glass) — the out-of-box look since 2026-10-30, replacing Harbor/Daybreak as the
       default pair. The owner asked for "exactly like Gboard" and picked the translucent style
       his own Gboard uses over a wallpaper: rounded tiles with a real gap, a see-through scrim
       so the app behind stays visible, and a modest backdrop blur where the platform offers one
       (`blurRadius`; the service applies it on API 31+, and the scrim alone carries the look
       everywhere else). Deliberately NOT copied: Google's icons, typeface, artwork or any
       Gboard asset — these are just geometry and colour. Id `gboard` resolves to the light or
       dark variant by the system's night mode, the way `monet` does. -->
  <style name="GboardLight" parent="BaseTheme">
    <item name="android:isLightTheme" tools:targetApi="29">true</item>
    <!-- 2026-11-04 (owner screenshot: "still looks ass" over Google Keep): the old field
         #CCF1F3F4 over a white app resolved to #F4F5F6 and the old key #B3FFFFFF resolved
         to plain white, so a 70 %-white key on a white field left the whole keyboard
         featureless. Gboard's light keyboard is a clearly grey field with white tiles on
         it. #F2 keeps a little of the app visible through the scrim (still "glass", the
         owner's chosen default) while landing on #E8EBEF over white, which is grey enough
         for an opaque white tile to read as a tile. -->
    <item name="colorKeyboard">#F2E7EAEE</item>
    <item name="colorKey">#FFFFFFFF</item>
    <!-- Pressed/activated was #FFFFFFFF = the resting tile, so a tap produced no visible
         change at all in light mode. Gboard darkens the tile it presses. -->
    <item name="colorKeyActivated">#FFDCDFE3</item>
    <item name="keyBorderWidth">0dp</item>
    <item name="keyBorderWidthActivated">0dp</item>
    <item name="keyBorderColorLeft">#00000000</item>
    <item name="keyBorderColorTop">#00000000</item>
    <item name="keyBorderColorRight">#00000000</item>
    <item name="keyBorderColorBottom">#00000000</item>
    <!-- 2026-11-05 (owner: "I compared them exactly"). Measured on his Gboard screenshot at the
         same 1080 px width: Gboard's tile corner arc is ~12-15 px ≈ 5 dp, on a 87x107 px tile.
         14 dp made every key a blob on a 33 dp-wide cell. 6 dp keeps a soft corner at Gboard's
         proportion instead of half the key width. -->
    <item name="keyBorderRadius">6dp</item>
    <item name="keyGap">3dp</item>
    <item name="blurRadius">24dp</item>
    <item name="colorLabel">#FF1F1F1F</item>
    <!-- Gboard does not recolour a pressed key's letter, and its first suggestion is told apart
         by weight, not hue: the bar highlight is colorLabelActivated, so it stays ink. -->
    <item name="colorLabelActivated">#FF1F1F1F</item>
    <item name="colorLabelLocked">#FF1A73E8</item>
    <item name="colorSubLabel">#FF5F6368</item>
    <!-- 2026-11-04: the strip is the keyboard's own field, as it is on Gboard — not a separate
         lighter band. It used to be an opaque #E7EAEE chosen to "land" on the field after the
         80 % opacity multiply; on top of the white apps the owner screenshots his suggestions
         sat on, that read as a second, paler row floating under the toolbar. -->
    <item name="colorSuggestionBar">#F2E7EAEE</item>
    <item name="suggestionBarMatchesKeyboard">true</item>
    <!-- Gboard's enter key: filled blue, white glyph, regardless of the theme's other key
